   "answer": "Based on the lecture material, here is a comparison of data parallelism and task parallelism, both of which are forms of parallelism where multiple jobs (instructions, operations, or tasks) are done concurrently [S1, p. 33].\n\n**Data Parallelism**\n*   **Core Concept:** Different pieces of data are operated on in parallel [S1, p. 33].\n*   **Implementation:** It involves vector processing and array processing [S1, p. 33].\n*   **Reasoning:** This approach focuses on applying the same operation to multiple data elements simultaneously.\n\n**Task Parallelism**\n*   **Core Concept:** Different tasks are operated on in parallel [S1, p. 33].\n*   **Implementation:** It is associated with multithreading [S1, p. 33].\n*   **Reasoning:** This approach focuses on executing distinct, separate tasks concurrently. The lecture further notes in the context of CPU multicore programming that this involves breaking down a complex task into multiple lightweight sub-tasks, where threads share the same virtual address space and global variables [S2, p. 55].\n\n**Summary of Differences**\n*   **Data Parallelism** operates on *different pieces of data* (e.g., vectors/arrays) [S1, p. 33].\n*   **Task Parallelism** operates on *different tasks* (e.g., multithreading) [S1, p. 33].\n\nBoth concepts are introduced in the context of moving from CPU to GPU to achieve huge computing power through multiprocessors [S1, p. 33].",
